export const devotionals56to69: Record<number, { body: string[]; reflection: string[] }> = {
  "56": {
    "body": [
      "Most of us know the feeling of an unopened message about something we would rather not face. A medical result, a family dispute, a letter from the tax office. We tell ourselves that we will deal with it later, and later has a way of becoming never. Nehemiah 1 begins with a man who did the opposite. He asked.",
      "The book opens with a signature. \"The words of Nehemiah son of Hakaliah\" (Nehemiah 1:1). We are now about a dozen years on from the end of Ezra, in the month of Kislev (roughly December) of the twentieth year of King Artaxerxes, around 445 BC. Nehemiah is \"in the citadel of Susa,\" one of the Persian royal centres, far from Jerusalem. Nothing in the text suggests that he was in any trouble. His is the safe address.",
      "Then his brother Hanani arrives from Judah with some other men. Nehemiah does not wait to be told. \"I questioned them about the Jewish remnant that had survived the exile, and also about Jerusalem\" (1:2). He asks about the people first and the city second. Both matter to him, even though he has presumably never lived there.",
      "The answer is blunt. \"Those who survived the exile and are back in the province are in great trouble and disgrace. The wall of Jerusalem is broken down, and its gates have been burned with fire\" (1:3). About seventy years after the temple was finished, the city that housed it still had no defences. A city without walls was exposed to raiders and open to contempt. \"Disgrace\" is the right word.",
      "The text does not tell us exactly when the wall fell. It may describe the damage done by Babylon in 586 BC, never repaired. It may include more recent damage, since Ezra 4:23 records a royal order that stopped the work by force. We should not choose too confidently. What matters is that the people of God, back in the land God promised, are still living among ruins.",
      "Nehemiah's reaction is the part that deserves our attention. \"When I heard these things, I sat down and wept. For some days I mourned and fasted and prayed before the God of heaven\" (1:4). The news changed him. He did not draft a plan or reach for a phone. He sat down, and for days he stayed with the grief.",
      "That is not passivity. Chapter 2 will show that he was doing a great deal of thinking in those weeks. But the order matters. Grief and prayer came before action. A leader who rushes to fix a problem he has not yet felt will tend to fix the wrong thing, or fix it badly.",
      "We should also notice who is weeping. Nehemiah is the king's own cupbearer, a trusted official with daily access to the most powerful man in the world. He could have decided that Jerusalem's troubles belonged to people who lived there. Instead he treats a ruined wall well over a thousand kilometres away as his own family's grief. Hanani is his brother, but the whole remnant is his people.",
      "There is a long biblical echo here. Jesus, coming to Jerusalem, \"wept over it\" (Luke 19:41). He saw a city that did not know what made for peace, and he did not look away from it. The tears of Nehemiah do not carry that weight, but they belong to the same family of compassion: the heart that is moved by the damage in God's people.",
      "Notice too the title he gives God: \"the God of heaven\" (1:4). It is the same name Cyrus used in his proclamation (Ezra 1:2), and it fits a man living in the capital of an earthly empire. Nehemiah has seen great power at close quarters, and he knows where the greater power sits.",
      "Nehemiah 1:4 also shows how little the story begins with strength. The man who will lead one of the most remarkable building projects in the Old Testament starts on the floor, weeping. Restoration in this book keeps starting with someone who cares enough to hurt."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What news about God's people, your church, your family or your own faith have you been avoiding? What would it look like to ask about it plainly this week, and to bring it to God before you try to fix it?"
    ]
  },
  "57": {
    "body": [
      "Nehemiah's prayer has a shape. It opens with who God is, moves to what we have done, then reminds God of what he has promised, and only at the very end does it ask for anything. Many of our own prayers run in the opposite direction.",
      "He also is not praying alone. He asks God to hear \"the prayer of your servants who delight in revering your name\" (1:11). Others were praying too, and Nehemiah knows it. The prayer he prays \"day and night\" (1:6) is the prayer of a community, and he is simply the one who happens to be near the king.",
      "\"Lord, the God of heaven, the great and awesome God, who keeps his covenant of love with those who love him and keep his commandments\" (1:5). Before Nehemiah mentions Jerusalem, he mentions God's character. The God he addresses is great, which means he is able, and he keeps covenant love, which means he is faithful. That combination is the reason the rest of the prayer makes sense.",
      "Then comes the confession. \"I confess the sins we Israelites, including myself and my father's family, have committed against you. We have acted very wickedly toward you\" (1:6–7). Notice the pronouns. Nehemiah was not alive when Jerusalem fell, and he could easily have blamed earlier generations. Yet he says \"we,\" and he names himself and his own family. Like Ezra before him, he will not pray as a spectator.",
      "The third movement is the most interesting. \"Remember the instruction you gave your servant Moses, saying, 'If you are unfaithful, I will scatter you among the nations, but if you return to me and obey my commands, then even if your exiled people are at the farthest horizon, I will gather them from there'\" (1:8–9). Nehemiah is quoting Scripture back to God. The words closely follow the promise of Deuteronomy 30:1–4, which he evidently knows well.",
      "This is not a way of pressuring God. It is a way of praying with confidence. God has already said what he intends to do for a people who return to him, and Nehemiah is simply holding the promise open in front of him. The prayer is only as secure as the word it rests on.",
      "Then he adds an argument from the exodus. \"They are your servants and your people, whom you redeemed by your great strength and your mighty hand\" (1:10). They belong to God because he has already bought them back once. A God who has done that is not likely to abandon his people to ruins.",
      "Only after all this does Nehemiah ask. \"Give your servant success today by granting him favor in the presence of this man\" (1:11). Then comes a short sentence that explains everything: \"I was cupbearer to the king.\" The request is small and specific. The \"man\" is Artaxerxes, and the favour Nehemiah wants is a hearing. A prayer this vast is attached to a request about one conversation.",
      "Notice how unspectacular this is. Nehemiah does not pray for a miracle on the walls. He prays for a good moment with a Persian king. Yet he prays it with God's whole history in view, and he expects to be part of the answer himself. His request puts him in the middle of the answer. He is not asking God to send someone else.",
      "We can learn a simple pattern from this. Start with who God is. Be honest about the sin that is yours to own, including the sin of the community you belong to. Hold on to what God has actually promised. Then ask for what you need, as specifically as you can. Jesus teaches something very close when he begins the Lord's Prayer with \"hallowed be your name\" before moving to bread, forgiveness and protection (Matthew 6:9–13)."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Think about the last few times you prayed about something heavy. How long did it take you to get to your request, and what came before it? Try beginning one prayer this week with what God has promised, in his own words, before you say anything about your need."
    ]
  },
  "58": {
    "body": [
      "Nehemiah's moment arrives in Nisan (roughly April), about four months after the news from Jerusalem. That is a long time to carry a burden and say nothing. It suggests, though the text does not say so, that he waited for the right opening and spent the months praying and thinking. When the chance comes, he is ready.",
      "\"When wine was brought for him, I took the wine and gave it to the king. I had not been sad in his presence before, so the king asked me, 'Why does your face look so sad when you are not ill? This can be nothing but sadness of heart.' I was very much afraid\" (2:1–2). Court etiquette in Persia seems to have expected cheerfulness in the royal presence. Sadness could look like discontent, and discontent could look like a threat. Nehemiah has been found out, and he is frightened.",
      "He answers with courtesy and truth. \"May the king live forever! Why should my face not look sad when the city where my ancestors are buried lies in ruins, and its gates have been destroyed by fire?\" (2:3). He does not mention Jerusalem by name, and he does not mention politics. He speaks of the graves of his ancestors, which a Persian king could readily understand.",
      "The king replies, \"What is it you want?\" (2:4). Here is the pivotal sentence of the passage. \"Then I prayed to the God of heaven, and I answered the king\" (2:4–5), the verse break falling between the two actions. There is no pause long enough for a prayer meeting. He prays in the space between the question and the answer. The long prayer of chapter 1 has made this short one possible.",
      "Then he asks, and the request is astonishing. \"Let him send me to the city in Judah where my ancestors are buried so that I can rebuild it\" (2:5). The same Artaxerxes, on the usual reading, had once ordered the building at Jerusalem to stop until he gave a further order (Ezra 4:21). Nehemiah is asking him, in effect, to reverse his own decision.",
      "The details that follow show how carefully he has thought. The king asks how long he will be away, and Nehemiah has an answer ready: \"It pleased the king to send me; so I set a time\" (2:6). Then he asks for letters to the governors of the provinces west of the Euphrates, \"so that they will provide me safe-conduct until I arrive in Judah,\" and for a letter to Asaph, keeper of the royal park, for timber for the gates, the city wall and his own residence (2:7–8).",
      "This is planning and prayer working together. Nehemiah did not assume that God would handle logistics without his involvement. He thought about borders, timber and travel time, and he prayed all the same. One detail is easy to miss. The queen is sitting beside the king when he asks how long Nehemiah will be away (2:6), and the request is granted. Yet Nehemiah does not credit the outcome to his own persuasive skill. \"And because the gracious hand of my God was on me, the king granted my requests\" (2:8).",
      "That phrase should sound familiar. It ran through Ezra's journey in chapters 7 and 8, and now it returns for Nehemiah. The king's heart is in God's hand, as Proverbs 21:1 puts it, and the same hand that moved Cyrus in Ezra 1 is moving Artaxerxes now.",
      "There is something worth noticing about the fear too. Nehemiah was very afraid, and he spoke anyway. Courage in Scripture is rarely the absence of fear. Here it is the willingness to speak the true thing while afraid, with a prayer on the lips and a plan in the pocket."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Is there a conversation you have been rehearsing for months? What might it look like to prepare it as carefully as Nehemiah did, and to pray the short prayer in the moment before you speak?"
    ]
  },
  "59": {
    "body": [
      "Nehemiah arrives in Jerusalem with letters, army officers and cavalry (2:9), and then does something that surprises us. He settles in and waits for three days. After that, he goes out at night with a handful of men and tells no one why.",
      "First, a word about the escort. The king \"had also sent army officers and cavalry with me\" (2:9). Compare Ezra, who had refused to ask for soldiers because he had told the king that God's hand was on those who seek him (Ezra 8:22). Nehemiah accepts the escort. Neither man is wrong. Both depend on God, and each acts on what his own situation seems to call for. Faith does not have a single costume.",
      "The first sign of opposition appears in the same passage. \"When Sanballat the Horonite and Tobiah the Ammonite official heard about this, they were very much disturbed that someone had come to promote the welfare of the Israelites\" (2:10). These two men will keep coming back. For now, notice what troubles them: not a threat to their safety but \"the welfare of the Israelites.\" They have a stake in Jerusalem staying weak.",
      "Nehemiah arrives, rests three days, and then \"I set out during the night with a few others. I had not told anyone what my God had put in my heart to do for Jerusalem. There were no mounts with me except the one I was riding on\" (2:12). The detail about the animals is practical. Presumably more animals would have meant more noise, and more noise would have meant more questions.",
      "He rides out through the Valley Gate and along the ruined wall, \"examining the walls of Jerusalem, which had been broken down, and its gates, which had been destroyed by fire\" (2:13). At one point the rubble is so thick that his mount cannot get through, so he goes up the valley, still looking (2:14–15). Finally he turns back and re-enters by the same gate.",
      "What is he doing? He is looking at the problem before he announces the solution. Hanani had told him the wall was broken, but a report is not the same as an assessment. Nehemiah needs to see which sections are down, how much timber will be needed, and where the work will begin. It is a survey, and he does it before he says a word to anyone. The phrase \"what my God had put in my heart\" matters here. He does not claim a voice from heaven. He describes a settled conviction that grew out of weeks of prayer, and he tests it against the actual stones.",
      "Jesus makes the same point in a parable. \"Suppose one of you wants to build a tower. Won't you first sit down and estimate the cost to see if you have enough money to complete it?\" (Luke 14:28). Counting the cost is not a lack of faith. Nehemiah's silence is not timidity either. It is the discipline of a man who wants to understand before he asks others to commit.",
      "The secrecy has a second purpose. \"The officials did not know where I had gone or what I was doing, because as yet I had said nothing to the Jews or the priests or nobles or officials or any others who would be doing the work\" (2:16). He has been given a burden by God. He will share it, but he will do so when he knows what he is asking.",
      "A doctor who examines the patient before prescribing is doing the same thing. The treatment may be urgent, and it may be obvious, but a wrong diagnosis wastes everyone's time. Nehemiah rides in the dark so that, in the morning, he can speak in specifics.",
      "Many of us would have arrived with a speech. Nehemiah arrives with questions, answers them with his own eyes, and only then looks for partners."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What project, decision or ministry are you feeling pressed to start? What would it mean to look carefully at the real situation first, and who might you need to listen to before you tell anyone your plan?"
    ]
  },
  "60": {
    "body": [
      "After the night ride, Nehemiah finally speaks. The room he addresses is the whole community: Jews, priests, nobles, officials, anyone who would be doing the work (2:16). What he says is short, honest and carefully built.",
      "\"Then I said to them, 'You see the trouble we are in: Jerusalem lies in ruins, and its gates have been burned with fire. Come, let us rebuild the wall of Jerusalem, and we will no longer be in disgrace'\" (2:17). Look at the pronouns again. It is \"the trouble we are in,\" and \"let us rebuild.\" Nehemiah has the king's backing and the king's letters, but he does not talk like a man handing down orders. He stands among them.",
      "He begins with what they can see for themselves. He does not have to convince the people that the wall is broken. They have lived with it for years. The shame of living in a defenceless city is something they already feel, and Nehemiah names it. A good leader often says aloud what people have quietly felt but not yet said.",
      "Then he gives them two reasons to act. \"I also told them about the gracious hand of my God on me and what the king had said to me\" (2:18). God's hand and the king's words are both offered as grounds for hope. The first is the deeper reason, and the second is the evidence. Nehemiah is not asking them to build on a hunch.",
      "The response is immediate. \"They replied, 'Let us start rebuilding.' So they began this good work\" (2:18). It is easy to skim past that line. A community that had lived with ruins for decades was ready to move once someone had spoken clearly and shown them why it could be done. The wall had been waiting for many things, including royal permission and timber, and now also for a community willing to say \"let us.\" God's providence, the king's letters and the people's renewed willingness all come together in that one verse.",
      "You see the same thing after a storm in a suburban street. Everyone stands at their gates looking at the fallen branches, and no one moves until one neighbour begins to haul. Within an hour the whole street is working. People often need permission to start more than they need instructions.",
      "The wall is called a \"good work\" (2:18), and this is no ordinary construction project. It guards the city that holds God's temple and carries his promises, and good work of that kind attracts opposition. The opposition arrives straight away. Sanballat, Tobiah and a third man, Geshem the Arab, \"mocked and ridiculed us. 'What is this you are doing?' they asked. 'Are you rebelling against the king?'\" (2:19). The accusation is calculated. It echoes the letter that stopped the work in Ezra 4, which also painted Jerusalem's rebuilding as rebellion. Nehemiah's permission answers the accusation. Rebellion is a serious charge at a Persian court, and the work had been stopped by royal order before (Ezra 4:23), so the threat is not empty. But the king's letters change the situation.",
      "His answer is the heart of the passage. \"The God of heaven will give us success. We his servants will start rebuilding, but as for you, you have no share in Jerusalem or any claim or historic right to it\" (2:20). He does not argue about politics. He rests the work on God, and he refuses to give the opponents a seat at the table.",
      "There is a lesson about confidence here. Nehemiah is not boasting about his own ability. The success is God's to give, and the workers are his servants. That is why he can speak so firmly. A work that rests on God's promise does not need the approval of those who want it to fail.",
      "There is also a lesson in how Nehemiah handles the mockery. He does not enter a long debate about rights or history. He gives one clear answer and returns to the wall. Opponents often want to drag the builders into an argument, because every hour spent arguing is an hour not spent building. Nehemiah answers once, and then he picks up his tools."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Who in your church or family may be waiting for someone to say \"let us start\"? What first, small action could you take this week to begin?"
    ]
  },
  "61": {
    "body": [
      "Nehemiah's story is about building and guarding, and Psalm 127 speaks to both. Its place here is thematic. Its title gives it to Solomon, builder of the temple, and it belongs to the Songs of Ascents (the pilgrim songs we met on Day 36). We cannot tell when it entered Israel's worship, but it sits well alongside the wall.",
      "\"Unless the LORD builds the house, the builders labor in vain. Unless the LORD watches over the city, the guards stand watch in vain\" (127:1). The verse does not say that building and guarding are pointless. It says they are in vain without God. Builders and guards are still at their posts. The point concerns what finally makes their work succeed.",
      "Look at how that fits Nehemiah. In chapter 4 he will do both things at once. \"But we prayed to our God and posted a guard day and night to meet this threat\" (4:9). The psalm does not tell Nehemiah to put down his trowel and wait. It tells him whom to rely on while he works.",
      "The second verse presses the point on a more personal level. \"In vain you rise early and stay up late, toiling for food to eat—for he grants sleep to those he loves\" (127:2). The target here is anxious toil: the kind of effort that comes from believing everything depends on us. Sleep is a quiet daily reminder that it does not. We stop each night, the world continues, and God holds it. Jesus himself slept in the stern of a boat during a storm (Mark 4:38), and the sleep of the one who built everything is a picture of the same trust.",
      "That is not an argument for laziness. The same psalm assumes builders, guards and people who work for their food. The Bible elsewhere warns against idleness (Proverbs 6:6–11). The contrast is between work done in trust and work done in panic. You can give a day's full effort and still go to bed at night.",
      "The psalm's language of house moves in the second half, where the subject turns to children. \"Children are a heritage from the LORD, offspring a reward from him\" (127:3). In Hebrew the word for house can mean a building or a family, and many commentators note that the words for build and sons sound alike. The psalm may be playing on that. God builds houses, in both senses.",
      "Verses 4 and 5 add a picture of security. A warrior with a quiver of arrows is ready for the moment when enemies gather at the city gate, where legal disputes were settled. The man in the picture is not ashamed in front of his opponents. His confidence comes from the people standing beside him, not from his own strength. The whole psalm describes a life held up by someone else.",
      "A word of care is needed. Verses 3 to 5 describe children as a gift in a general way, as wisdom literature does. They are not a formula that guarantees a full quiver for every faithful couple. Many people who love God have struggled with infertility or loss, and the psalm is not pointing a finger at them. It teaches that life is received from God before it is achieved.",
      "For Christians the psalm reaches its fullest meaning in a promise from Jesus. \"On this rock I will build my church, and the gates of Hades will not overcome it\" (Matthew 16:18). The one who builds the house is Christ himself. Our work in his church is real, and it is also his.",
      "That is the quiet confidence behind Nehemiah's wall. The builders are named, the guards are posted, and the whole project depends on a God who watches over the city."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What are you working at so hard that you have stopped trusting God with it? Tonight, as you go to sleep, try saying quietly that this work is his, and see what changes by morning."
    ]
  },
  "62": {
    "body": [
      "Chapter 3 looks like a roster. It lists who built which section of wall, gate by gate, with family names and fathers' names and the occasional job description. A reader could be forgiven for skimming it. But its opening details are worth examining.",
      "The first is at the very beginning. \"Eliashib the high priest and his fellow priests went to work and rebuilt the Sheep Gate. They dedicated it and set its doors in place\" (3:1). The spiritual leaders go first. The gate lay on the north side of the city near the temple area, which suits the priests. The wall does not wait for volunteers. The people whose job is to lead worship begin with a hammer.",
      "Notice what they do with the gate. They \"dedicated it,\" setting it apart for God. This is not only a construction job but an act of worship. The wall will be a defence against enemies, and it will also be a wall around a holy city. That is why a high priest is willing to carry stones.",
      "The chapter then moves around the wall, section by section, in a spirit of cooperation. \"The men of Jericho built the adjoining section, and Zakkur son of Imri built next to them\" (3:2). The repeated phrase \"next to\" runs through the chapter like a thread. The wall is one wall, but it is built in many hands. No one sees the whole of it, and no one is asked to.",
      "Then we meet the people. \"Uzziel son of Harhaiah, one of the goldsmiths, repaired the next section\" (3:8). We might not expect a goldsmith to be good at rough stone and mortar. Yet he is on the wall. A little later, \"Shallum son of Hallohesh, ruler of a half-district of Jerusalem, repaired the next section with the help of his daughters\" (3:12). A district leader puts his own hands to the work, and his daughters join him. The text does not explain why this is worth noting. It does not need to.",
      "The chapter also has a moment of failure. \"The next section was repaired by the men of Tekoa, but their nobles would not put their shoulders to the work under their supervisors\" (3:5). The ordinary men of Tekoa worked, and in verse 27 they take a second section, while their leaders held back. It is a short sentence with a long reach. Some people with the most status and the most to offer are not willing to do the job.",
      "It is worth pausing on Eliashib. The high priest of chapter 3 is probably the same man who appears in chapter 13, where his household becomes tangled up with Tobiah, one of the wall's enemies. We will come to that later. For now, the point is simply that a good beginning does not guarantee a good ending. We need God's help to finish what we start.",
      "The message of the roster is straightforward. God's work is carried by names. The book spends a whole chapter honouring the people who actually did it. The same thing is true of the gates. Each one is named, rebuilt and set in place, because the city they guard is the one where God's people worship him. Notice too that no section is called the most important. Gate by gate the text gives the same treatment to every worker, and the same two words, repaired and next to, carry the whole chapter. A church today is built the same way, by a goldsmith with a trowel, a district leader with his daughters, a priest at the gate. Their names matter to God even when no one else remembers them."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "If a chapter were written about your church's work, where would your name appear, and what section would it say you repaired? Is there a task you have been leaving to someone else that you could pick up this month?"
    ]
  },
  "63": {
    "body": [
      "The second half of the wall list carries on in the same manner as the first, and a reader might be tempted to treat it as the dull part of the book. It is worth slowing down, because the details are the point. The text records who built, where they built, and in several cases how they went about it.",
      "Watch for a small phrase that returns again and again: \"in front of his house.\" \"Beyond them, Benjamin and Hasshub made repairs in front of their house, and next to them, Azariah son of Maaseiah, the son of Ananiah, made repairs beside his house\" (3:23). Later, \"Above the Horse Gate, the priests made repairs, each in front of his own house\" (3:28). A few verses on, \"Zadok son of Immer made repairs opposite his house\" (3:29).",
      "Many Jerusalem families, then, repaired the stretch of wall nearest their own front door. That is a sensible arrangement. A man has the strongest reason to keep a wall sound at the place where his family sleeps. It also means the work began for them not as an abstract project but as the protection of their own households. A wall is easier to build when you can see what it is guarding. In the end it guards more than houses. It protects the city where God's people gather to worship him.",
      "There is a gentle lesson in that for a church. Some of the work God gives us sits right at the front door. The neighbour next to us, the children under our roof, the friend at the next desk: these are our sections of wall. We may long for a larger project elsewhere. The chapter honours the repairs made opposite one's own house.",
      "Then comes a name with a single descriptor. \"Next to him, Baruch son of Zabbai zealously repaired another section from the angle to the entrance of the house of Eliashib the high priest\" (3:20). Baruch is the one man whose work is described with an adverb. The text does not tell us what was special about the way he worked. It simply notes that he was zealous, and the book preserved the remark.",
      "Further along we meet \"Shemaiah son of Shekaniah, the guard at the East Gate\" (3:29). Another man is \"Hananiah son of Shelemiah, and Hanun, the sixth son of Zalaph\" (3:30). That last description is a lovely detail. Hanun is remembered as the sixth son. Why the book notes it we are not told, but it shows how personally the list was kept.",
      "The chapter reads like a roll of honour, and the people on it are largely ordinary. We know almost nothing about any of them beyond a father's name and a position on the wall. But their names are in the Bible, which means God wanted them recorded. The work of a Christian community has the same shape. Most of it is done by people whose names will never appear in a history book.",
      "Paul uses a different image for the same truth. \"The eye cannot say to the hand, 'I don't need you!'\" (1 Corinthians 12:21). The point is that the body has many parts, and no part is dispensable. Nehemiah 3 is a wall made of that principle. The goldsmith, the guard, the sixth son and the priest each cover a few metres, and the wall stands only if all of them do.",
      "There is a quiet warning too. A wall with one missing section is still open. If the men of one family had decided that their stretch was not worth the effort, the whole city would have been exposed at that point. The strength of the wall is the strength of its weakest section.",
      "This also explains the gaps in the record. Some sections are described in a single line, and some workers are known only by their town. The chapter does not rank them. It trusts that the whole wall, rather than any one stretch, is what God is honouring.",
      "So the chapter closes on a note of shared work. No one finishes the wall alone, and no one is asked to. Each repairs what is within reach, next to someone else doing the same."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What is the section of wall nearest your own front door? Is there something within your reach this month that you have been waiting for someone more qualified to do?"
    ]
  },
  "64": {
    "body": [
      "The wall is going up, and the opposition changes its method. Sanballat and his friends have already mocked the builders and accused them of rebellion (2:19). Now the mockery grows louder and more public. Mockery is cheaper than an army, and it often works.",
      "In the presence of his associates and the army of Samaria, Sanballat says, \"What are those feeble Jews doing? Will they restore their wall? Will they offer sacrifices? Will they finish in a day? Can they bring the stones back to life from those heaps of rubble—burned as they are?\" (4:2). The sentences come fast, each a jab. They call the builders feeble, doubt their ability, laugh at their religious devotion, and sneer at the quality of the stones. The exact force of \"Will they offer sacrifices?\" is debated. It may be a jibe at their piety.",
      "Tobiah joins in. \"What they are building—even a fox climbing up on it would break down their wall of stones!\" (4:3). It is a good joke, which is why it hurts. Ridicule is rarely accurate, but it picks up something real: the wall is rough, and the builders are tired. Mockery works by turning a true weakness into a reason to stop.",
      "Nehemiah's reaction is surprising. He does not argue, and he does not retaliate. He prays. \"Hear us, our God, for we are despised. Turn their insults back on their own heads\" (4:4). Then he asks for the opponents to be given over as plunder, and prays that God will not \"cover up their guilt or blot out their sins from your sight\" (4:5).",
      "These are hard words, and they belong to the family of prayers called imprecatory, which we met on Day 2. We should not simply copy them. Christians can ask God to bring justice, and we are also told to love our enemies and pray for those who persecute us (Matthew 5:44). Nehemiah does not take revenge himself. He takes his anger to God and leaves the verdict with him. The New Testament carries the same instinct further. Of Jesus under abuse, Peter writes, \"he entrusted himself to him who judges justly\" (1 Peter 2:23).",
      "Notice what comes next. \"So we rebuilt the wall till all of it reached half its height, for the people worked with all their heart\" (4:6). The prayer is not the end of the effort. It frees them to carry on. The old King James Version renders the last phrase memorably: \"for the people had a mind to work.\" The Hebrew speaks of the heart, and a mind to work is a good way to describe it. The work was not driven by anger at the mockers. It was driven by a shared commitment to the wall.",
      "That is a useful pattern for anyone who has been laughed at for something good. The goal is not to prove the critics wrong in an argument. It is to hand the matter to God and keep building. Mockery loses much of its force when the people it targets refuse to stop.",
      "There is a further detail worth noticing in the order of events. Nehemiah's prayer comes before the half-height wall, not after it. He did not wait to see whether the mockery would stop. He gave it to God while the stones were still lying in the rubble, and the work moved forward in the meantime. Prayer here is not a reward for success. It is part of how the work continues.",
      "We live with a louder version of this today. A comment section can make any serious effort look foolish in a few lines. Many of the people who write those lines will never lift a stone. The wall, meanwhile, rises half its height while the commentary goes on.",
      "None of this means the criticism is always empty. Sometimes a fox really could knock the wall down, and a good builder listens for truth in the sneer. But the sneer of Sanballat was not offered to improve the wall."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "When someone has mocked something you were trying to do for God, what did you do with the hurt? What might it look like to pray as Nehemiah did and then simply get back to work?"
    ]
  },
  "65": {
    "body": [
      "Ridicule has failed, so the enemies escalate. The wall has reached half its height, and the gaps are closing. At that point the opposition grows from words to a plot, and it spreads beyond Sanballat. Now Tobiah, the Arabs, the Ammonites and the people of Ashdod are all angry, and \"they all plotted together to come and fight against Jerusalem and stir up trouble against it\" (4:8).",
      "Nehemiah's response is brief and balanced. \"But we prayed to our God and posted a guard day and night to meet this threat\" (4:9). He does both. He prays, and he posts guards. Psalm 127 taught that the guards stand watch in vain unless the LORD watches over the city, and here a man puts it into practice without making a speech about it.",
      "But the pressure now comes from inside the community as well. Judah's own workers begin to complain. \"The strength of the laborers is giving out, and there is so much rubble that we cannot rebuild the wall\" (4:10). It is a very human sentence. They are not rebelling. They are exhausted. The first glow of the project has gone, the rubble is still everywhere, and the end is nowhere in sight.",
      "Anyone who has been through a long renovation knows this stage. Nothing in the text suggests that these workers lacked faith. Tired people tell the truth about their limits, and Nehemiah does not rebuke them for it. In week three the dust has stopped being exciting, and the walls are still bare. Many projects fail not at the start or the end but in the middle, when the energy is gone and the finish is still far away. The same is true of church life, of marriages and of long-term discipleship.",
      "At the same time, fear spreads from the surrounding villages. Jews who lived near the enemy territory repeatedly warned that attacks could come from every direction (4:12). The enemies themselves were planning a surprise attack designed to kill the workers and stop the work (4:11). Fear and fatigue feed each other, and together they can shut a project down without a single blow being struck.",
      "Nehemiah's answer is practical first. He stations people by families behind the lowest and most exposed parts of the wall, with swords, spears and bows (4:13). The people who will defend each section are the ones with the most to lose in it, which is a clever use of human loyalty. More is at stake than family safety, since this is the city God chose as a dwelling for his Name (1:9), and the promises he made to gather his people are tied to it. Then he stands and speaks. The text says he \"looked things over,\" and the timing matters. He speaks after he has dealt with the practical danger, not instead of it. Courage that is only a speech tends to fade, while courage with a plan behind it lasts.",
      "\"Don't be afraid of them. Remember the Lord, who is great and awesome, and fight for your families, your sons and your daughters, your wives and your homes\" (4:14). There are two commands in that verse: remember, and fight. And the words about God deserve a second look. \"Great and awesome\" is how Nehemiah addressed God in his prayer in chapter 1. The truth he held on to in private months ago is now the truth he gives to a frightened crowd. A man who has prayed it first can say it with weight.",
      "That is how courage often works. We are not taught to feel braver. We are told to remember who God is. Fear shrinks the world down to the next threat. Remembering enlarges it again to include God, and from that wider view the enemies look smaller.",
      "The New Testament gives the same medicine for weariness. \"Consider him who endured such opposition from sinners, so that you will not grow weary and lose heart\" (Hebrews 12:3). Looking at Christ does not remove the rubble. It changes what we think of it."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "When you feel yourself giving out in the middle of something that matters, what do you tend to fix your eyes on? What truth about God could you deliberately recall, as Nehemiah told his people to?"
    ]
  },
  "66": {
    "body": [
      "Nehemiah's words steady the people, and then the enemy's plan collapses. \"When our enemies heard that we were aware of their plot and that God had frustrated it, we all returned to the wall, each to our own work\" (4:15). The sentence holds two explanations side by side. The plot fails because it has been discovered, and the text says that God frustrated it. Both are said to be true, and neither cancels the other.",
      "Having won the moment, Nehemiah does not relax. \"From that day on, half of my men did the work, while the other half were equipped with spears, shields, bows and armor\" (4:16). Half work and half guard. The text then describes two groups at work. \"Those who carried materials did their work with one hand and held a weapon in the other, and each of the builders wore his sword at his side as he worked\" (4:17–18).",
      "It is a vivid picture. The carriers haul material with one hand free for a weapon, and the builders set stones with a sword at their side. Nothing about this is relaxed. The wall is being built in the shadow of a threat, and everyone has accepted that the work must go on regardless.",
      "Then the trumpet. \"But the man who sounded the trumpet stayed with me\" (4:18). The wall is long and the workers are spread out, so Nehemiah sets up a signal. \"Wherever you hear the sound of the trumpet, join us there. Our God will fight for us!\" (4:20). In one verse we have a practical system and a theological claim. The system gets people to the right place quickly. The claim tells them whose battle it is.",
      "Notice that the claim does not replace the system. God will fight for them, and so they carry swords. This is how the book keeps handling the relationship between divine action and human effort. It never accepts the idea that trusting God means doing nothing, and it never accepts the idea that careful preparation can replace trust.",
      "The cost of all this is real. People stay inside Jerusalem at night so they can serve as guards by night and workers by day. \"Neither I nor my brothers nor my men nor the guards with me took off our clothes; each had his weapon, even when he went for water\" (4:23). Nehemiah himself joins them. He asks nothing of his men that he does not accept for himself. A leader who sleeps in his clothes beside his workers earns the right to tell them to keep going.",
      "Volunteers who patrol Sydney's beaches know something about this combination. They train, they watch and they keep their equipment close, and they stay alert while the flags are up. Most of the time nothing happens. They stay ready anyway, and when something does happen, the readiness is the whole point.",
      "We must be careful about applying this passage. Nehemiah was acting under royal authority, and the situation was that of a community with homes and families under threat. The church is not a city-state. Paul is explicit that \"the weapons we fight with are not the weapons of the world\" (2 Corinthians 10:4). Our battle is fought with truth, prayer and patient love. Ephesians 6 describes it in the language of armour, and the armour is made of truth, righteousness, faith and the word of God.",
      "But the shape of Nehemiah's response still teaches us. The people of God keep working under threat. They stay alert, they stay together, and they hold on to the confidence that the battle belongs to God. Building with weapons ready describes more than a day on a wall. It describes a faith that is both busy and watchful."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Most of us lean one way: all work and no watchfulness, or all vigilance and no building. Which is your lean? What would it look like to hold both?"
    ]
  },
  "67": {
    "body": [
      "The enemies outside the wall have been dealt with for now. Then a different cry rises from inside. \"Now the men and their wives raised a great outcry against their fellow Jews\" (5:1). The threat in Nehemiah 5 is not Sanballat. It is the community itself.",
      "The chapter gives us three groups of complaint, and it is worth hearing them one at a time. First, a shortage of food. \"We and our sons and daughters are numerous; in order for us to eat and stay alive, we must get grain\" (5:2). Second, loans against land. \"We are mortgaging our fields, our vineyards and our homes to get grain during the famine\" (5:3). Third, taxes. \"We have had to borrow money to pay the king's tax on our fields and vineyards\" (5:4).",
      "The pattern is a familiar one. Famine pushes families into debt. They borrow from richer neighbours, those neighbours take their land as security, and the king's tax collector still arrives. The families are working on the wall while their farms slip away. The wall may be going up, but their private lives are coming down. Anyone who has watched a family sink under a mortgage while the neighbours carry on as usual will recognise the pressure.",
      "Then the cry reaches its worst point. \"Although we are of the same flesh and blood as our fellow Jews and though our children are as good as theirs, yet we have to subject our sons and daughters to slavery. Some of our daughters have already been enslaved, but we are powerless, because our fields and our vineyards belong to others\" (5:5). The very people who are working to rebuild the covenant city are being forced to hand their children over to pay the debts.",
      "This should shock us. God's law for Israel had never allowed lenders to profit from the poverty of their own people. The Law forbade charging interest to a fellow Israelite in need (see Exodus 22:25 and Leviticus 25:35–37), and it limited the way debt could turn into slavery. What the book is describing is a community that had forgotten those provisions at exactly the moment when it was rebuilding its identity.",
      "The wording of the complaint matters. \"Of the same flesh and blood\" is a claim about equality. The poor are not a different class. They are brothers and sisters. A people who share an exodus ought to share a table, and the complaint is that they no longer do.",
      "This is also a reminder about where trouble tends to arrive. A church or community that spends all its energy on threats from outside can miss a quiet crisis in its own kitchen. The enemies on the hills were visible. The injustice at home was easy to overlook, especially for those who profited by it. Opposition can make us loyal to the cause and blind to the people it is for.",
      "There is a very old echo in the idea of an outcry. In Egypt, \"the Israelites groaned in their slavery and cried out\" and God heard them (Exodus 2:23–24). Now the cry rises from the people God redeemed, directed against members of their own community. The question of the chapter is whether anyone will hear it.",
      "Notice the shape of the complaint. The poor do not ask for charity. They ask for fairness, on the grounds that they belong to the same people. Scripture treats the demand as legitimate, and it expects those with power to take it seriously.",
      "Wall-building and justice are not rivals. If the community defended its city and neglected the poor, the wall would be a monument to the wrong thing. God's people are to be a community whose strength protects the weak."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Which person around you is quietly paying a cost that others have not noticed? What would it take for you to hear their cry before it becomes an outcry?"
    ]
  },
  "68": {
    "body": [
      "When Nehemiah hears the outcry, he is angry. \"When I heard their outcry and these charges, I was very angry\" (5:6). Anger is not necessarily a fault. Here it is aimed at injustice, in defence of people who cannot defend themselves. But Nehemiah does not act on it at once.",
      "\"I pondered them in my mind and then accused the nobles and officials. I told them, 'You are charging your own people interest!' So I called together a large meeting to deal with them\" (5:7). The order matters. First he thinks. Then he speaks to the people who are responsible, and he does it in public. There is a pause between the anger and the action, and the pause gives the anger a purpose. Anger that rushes out usually does damage. Anger that stops to think can do justice. James says that human anger does not produce the righteousness God desires (James 1:20), and Nehemiah's pause is part of what keeps his anger from falling into that category.",
      "Notice who he confronts. The nobles and officials are the same men who have been working on the wall. They are the community's leaders, and they have a great deal of power. Nehemiah is a newcomer with the king's letters, and he now has to correct them. It is a risk. If the nobles walk away, the project may fall apart.",
      "His argument is moral and historical. \"As far as possible, we have bought back our fellow Jews who were sold to the Gentiles. Now you are selling your own people, only for them to be sold back to us!\" (5:8). The nobles are doing the opposite of redemption. The community had been spending money to rescue Jews from slavery abroad, and some of its leaders were creating new slaves at home. The leaders have nothing to say.",
      "Then comes a question that cuts to the root. \"What you are doing is not right. Shouldn't you walk in the fear of our God to avoid the reproach of our Gentile enemies?\" (5:9). Two reasons are offered. The first is reverence for God. The second is the community's witness among the nations. Injustice inside the church damages the church's testimony outside it.",
      "Then Nehemiah does something that makes his words harder to dismiss. \"I and my brothers and my men are also lending the people money and grain. But let us stop charging interest!\" (5:10). He puts himself in the picture and does not exempt himself from the call. The text does not say that he had been charging the poor exploitative interest, and we should not assume a confession it does not record. The point is that he joins the community's change of course, and the demand has more force because he asks nothing that he is unwilling to do.",
      "The demand itself is concrete. \"Give back to them immediately their fields, vineyards, olive groves and houses, and also the interest you are charging them\" (5:11). This is restitution, and it is costly. The nobles lose both property and income. The one percent mentioned in the verse is probably a monthly rate, which adds up quickly over a year. It is a reminder that repentance in the Bible often has a financial side. When Zacchaeus met Jesus, he said, \"If I have cheated anybody out of anything, I will pay back four times the amount\" (Luke 19:8).",
      "The nobles agree. \"We will give it back. And we will not demand anything more from them. We will do as you say\" (5:12). Nehemiah makes them swear before the priests, and then he shakes out the folds of his robe: \"In this way may God shake out of their house and possessions anyone who does not keep this promise\" (5:13). The dramatic gesture says that a promise before God is a serious thing.",
      "The result is a very good one. \"At this the whole assembly said, 'Amen,' and praised the Lord. And the people did as they had promised\" (5:13). A confrontation that could have split the community instead ends in praise. It shows that correction, done with humility and backed by action, can bring a community together."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Who is the person you have been avoiding a hard conversation with because it would cost something? What would it look like to think carefully, speak plainly and include yourself in the standard you set?"
    ]
  },
  "69": {
    "body": [
      "Nehemiah has told the nobles to give back what they should never have taken. Now he describes a different kind of sacrifice: refusing benefits he could legitimately have claimed. The nobles were required to make restitution. Nehemiah chose to practise generosity. His account reads like a personal ledger, and it explains why his challenge was credible.",
      "\"Moreover, from the twentieth year of King Artaxerxes . . . neither I nor my brothers ate the food allotted to the governor\" (5:14). The span runs twelve years, from the twentieth to the thirty-second year of the king, roughly 445 to 433 BC. For that entire time, Nehemiah did not claim the food allowance that went with his office. He was entitled to it. Governors were provided for by the people they governed, and he had a right to expect it.",
      "The contrast is with those who came before him. \"The earlier governors—those preceding me—placed a heavy burden on the people\" (5:15). Nehemiah does not repeat the pattern. His reason is given in a single line that could stand as a motto for the whole chapter: \"out of reverence for God I did not act like that.\" The fear of God, for Nehemiah, is not only a feeling in worship. It changes how a man uses power.",
      "He adds that he \"devoted myself to the work on this wall\" (5:16), and that his men were also busy with the work. He did not use his position to build up property for himself. Reading between the lines, there was plenty of opportunity, and nothing in the chapter suggests he was ever short of the chance. The chapter has just told us that land was changing hands in a famine. Nehemiah chose to give his time to the wall instead.",
      "His generosity went further than restraint. \"A hundred and fifty Jews and officials ate at my table\" (5:17), and the daily provision was large: \"one ox, six choice sheep and some poultry\" (5:18). He fed a crowd at his own expense. \"I never demanded the food allotted to the governor, because the demands were heavy on these people\" (5:18). He gave up an income, and he spent his own money on the community.",
      "This is a picture of leadership that costs the leader. In Paul's letters we find the same instinct. Paul defended the right of ministers to be supported, and then, in Corinth, he gave it up so as not to place an obstacle in the way of the gospel (1 Corinthians 9:12; we met this on Day 44). A right is a real thing, and a mature person may still decide not to use it.",
      "Jesus is the final form of this pattern. \"For even the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many\" (Mark 10:45). Nehemiah is a shadow of that. He is a flawed man who gave up an income. Christ is the king who gave himself.",
      "The chapter ends with a prayer. \"Remember me with favor, my God, for all I have done for these people\" (5:19). Some readers wince at that. Does Nehemiah think God owes him? It helps to remember the earlier prayer of chapter 1, where he confessed his own sins and his family's. The word \"favor\" is a request for kindness. It is not a demand for payment. He asks to be remembered, and he leaves the reward to God.",
      "This prayer also recurs. Nehemiah will ask God to remember him again in chapter 13, which shows how much he relies on God's judgment rather than the people's applause.",
      "The prayer is also very honest. Nehemiah does not pretend that no one will notice his sacrifice. He brings the matter to the only one who can weigh it."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Which right, comfort or payment are you entitled to that you could quietly lay down for the sake of someone else? What would it look like to do it for reverence for God, and without needing anyone to notice?"
    ]
  }
};
